      "description": "Provost's Faculty Fellows in Artificial Intelligence (two) - San Diego, California, United States Skip to main content Why USD Benefits and Compensation Already Applied? My Profile Kiosk mode Employment Opportunities at USD Provost's Faculty Fellows in Artificial Intelligence (two) San Diego, California Faculty Full-time regular Closing on: Nov 15 2026 5756 Position Title & Department: Provost's Faculty Fellows in Artificial Intelligence (two) Posting # 5756 Department Description: The mission of Office of the Provost and Vice President for Academic Affairs at USD is to provide academic leadership to its college and schools and promote excellence in all of the university's academic programs and initiatives, in order to educate ethical and compassionate leaders. University Description: The University of San Diego, a contemporary and engaged Roman Catholic institution , was founded by the Diocese of San Diego and the Society of the Sacred Heart in 1949. Governed by an independent board of trustees since 1972, USD remains committed to a liberal arts education grounded in the Catholic intellectual tradition and the pursuit of truth, goodness and beauty. Inspired by this centuries old tradition of Catholic higher education, the University welcomes people of all faith traditions and any, or no, religious background. The future success of USD relies on the contributions of those who seek to foster the development of engaged global citizens and an earnest confrontation of humanity’s urgent challenges . Detailed Description: This recruitment is open to current University of San Diego tenured faculty only. Two Provost's AI Faculty Fellows will work closely with the Provost's team focusing on designing strategies and implementing initiatives to support and advance the university's approach to AI in alignment with the Pope Leo XIV’s encyclical Magnifica Humanitas . This position will play an important role in thoughtfully implementing USD’s approach to AI in a way that aligns with our mission, vision, and values, and serves our entire community, especially students, faculty, and staff. Key responsibilities include: Clarity & Guidance: Support the development of institutional guidance and policy on the responsible use of AI in teaching, learning, and administrative practice, and collaborate with faculty, General Counsel, HR, and University Senate as appropriate. Foster Ongoing Dialogue: Collaborate with campus partners to organize speakers and support department- and unit-level conversations that advance campus-wide dialogue on AI. Ensuring Mission Alignment: Support the assessment of enterprise AI tools and resources, and contribute to institutional reporting that tracks USD's progress on AI initiatives. Incentivize Ideation: Contribute to the design and administration of internal grant programs that support faculty and staff exploration of AI, and encourage faculty-student research partnerships to examine the implications of AI within their disciplines. The Provost's Faculty Fellows reports directly to and works under the general supervision of the Associate Provost for Research and Administration. The appointment will start as soon as November 15, 2026. This position is renewable on an annual basis for up to two years. Each Faculty Fellow will serve an estimated one month in the summer, with a teaching load of one 3-unit course within their respective departments per each Fall and Spring semester. Each Faculty Fellow is expected to remain active in their ongoing faculty research and scholarship agenda. Job Requirements: Full-time faculty at USD. Strong familiarity with the university's academic and administrative structures. “Institutional-mindedness” as well as demonstrated trust and credibility among colleagues across the university. This position requires clear familiarity with AI tools and techniques alongside institutional judgment, trust, and the ability to work alongside others to move initiatives forward. Additionally, note that given the expectations required of this role including continued scholarship and teaching responsibilities, it is expected that the fellow will not engage in outside employment or consulting for the duration of the appointment. Special Application Instructions: Click the \"Apply Now\" button to complete our online application. In addition, please upload a cover letter and CV to your application profile for the hiring managers’ review. Review of applications will begin immediately with a priority deadline of November 15, 2026 . Note, however, that the job posting will be up for at least three days. After that time, applications will be reviewed by the hiring manager/committee throughout the posting period. A candidate may be selected at any time which could then close this posting on a date earlier than listed. Contact: Dr. Austin Choi-Fitzpatrick, caustin@sandiego.edu If you have any technical difficulties, please contact the Employment Services Team at jobs@sandiego.edu Recommended Jobs Student Life Coordinator San Diego, California Staff, Student Affairs The Student Life Coordinator provides coordination of TRS and other student facing programs and administrative support to the Assistant Vice President for Student Life. Support includes managing calendars, incoming calls, email, and written correspondence as requested. Handles highly confidential information and materials. Public Safety Officer San Diego, California Public Safety, Staff Provides a wide range of services to the University community including crime prevention, crime control, parking control and furnishing aid and information to the campus community and visitors. Senior Program Coordinator, Carnegie Elective Classification for Community Engagement San Diego, California School of Leadership and Education Sciences, Staff The University of San Diego, School of Leadership and Education Sciences (SOLES), in partnership with the Carnegie Foundation for the Advancement of Teaching, hosts the long-established Carnegie Elective Classification for Community Engagement. The Community Engagement Classification serves as the leading framework for higher education community engagement work and is widely used to establish benchmarks and criteria for effectively institutionalizing community engagement across diverse institutional contexts. The Senior Program Coordinator provides essential administrative, logistical, and front-line support that keeps the classification's day-to-day operations running smoothly. Facebook LinkedIn Instagram X © 2026 University of San Diego. All rights reserved. Cookie Preferences Web Privacy Web Accessibility Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
